Troubleshooting Tips
BIND9 fails to start:

Check configuration syntax: sudo named-checkconf
Verify zone file syntax: sudo named-checkzone example.local /etc/bind/db.example.local
Review logs: sudo journalctl -u bind9 -n 50
Python script cannot resolve domains:

Verify DNS server is running: sudo systemctl status bind9
Test manual resolution: dig @localhost example.local
Check firewall rules: sudo ufw status
Elasticsearch connection refused:

Verify service status: sudo systemctl status elasticsearch
Check if port is listening: sudo netstat -tlnp | grep 9200
Review logs: sudo journalctl -u elasticsearch -n 50
Zeek not capturing traffic:

Verify interface configuration in /opt/zeek/etc/node.cfg
Check Zeek status: sudo /opt/zeek/bin/zeekctl status
Review Zeek logs: tail -f /opt/zeek/logs/current/stderr.log
Conclusion
This lab demonstrated comprehensive DNS security monitoring using multiple tools. You implemented DNSSEC for cryptographic validation, developed custom monitoring scripts for change detection, and deployed enterprise-grade monitoring with Zeek and ELK stack. These techniques form the foundation of DNS hijacking defense in production environments. Understanding DNS security is critical as DNS remains a primary attack vector for network compromise.

Key takeaways:

DNSSEC provides cryptographic assurance of DNS data integrity
Continuous monitoring detects unauthorized DNS changes
Network traffic analysis reveals DNS-based attacks
Visualization aids in identifying anomalous patterns
Defense-in-depth requires multiple complementary security layers
